val. Its holding costs are also the highest.\n- **Candidate 4:** over-orders by about 107 units of product 0 and about 85 of product 1. Around day 20 the warehouse overflows by roughly 60 units. The unsold stock at the end is pure loss.\n- **Candidate 2:** orders below average demand, so each cycle runs short. That's about 70 units of product 0 at 7 each (lost margin plus penalty).\n\n**Candidate 0 vs. Candidate 1:**\n- **Candidate 0:** its product 0 buffer grows to about 30, which is good. But its product 1 orders have almost no safety margin, so it loses about 30 units (about 180) across the cycles. That includes the day-22 spike (forecast 61), where it has only 110 against an expected 109. On day 20 the warehouse also slightly overflows, by about 3 units.\n- **Candidate 1:** its buffer grows steadily for both products, to about 18–19 units each. Product 0 is a bit weaker early (about 40 more lost than Candidate 0), but product 1 is much stronger (about 110 fewer lost). Holding and fees come out roughly even with Candidate 0. Its batches are small, so the warehouse never fills, and it handles the day-22 spike better.\n\nBoth plans buy almost the same amount in total (about 9,115 each), so the difference comes from Candidate 1's better buffer for product 1 and its resilience to the randomness in demand."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 360, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5754, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 360}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-26T08:34:51.566Z", "request_id": "req_011CgQYEA1Pb27BBMgoHz3uD"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"